#### Inclusive electron identification

The inclusive electrons are required to be identified by combining the information measured by TPC and TOF. TPC is able to distinguish different particle species by measuring particle ionization energy loss (\(dE/dx\)) in the TPC gas (Eq 2.1). Experimentally, the normalized \(dE/dx\), \(n\sigma_{x}\) (x can be \(\pi\), K, p, e etc.) is widely used. The definition of which is shown in Eq. 3.4:

\[n\sigma_{x}=\frac{1}{R}\ln\frac{\left\langle{d\,E/dx}\right\rangle_{measured}}{ \left\langle{d\,E/dx}\right\rangle_{x}} \tag{3.4}\]

where \(\left\langle{d\,E/dx}\right\rangle_{measured}\) is the measured track mean \(\left\langle{d\,E/dx}\right\rangle\) and \(\left\langle{d\,E/dx}\right\rangle_{x}\) is expected mean \(\left\langle{d\,E/dx}\right\rangle\) if assuming the track is particle x, and R is STAR TPC ln(dE/dx) resolution, depending on number of hits in the TPC used for dE/dx measurement (nHits-Dedx), pseudo-rapidity of the track, momentum of the track, TPC gas component and so on. Combining with the track information provided by TPC, the TOF system are able to identify particles with the same momentum but different masses according to the inverse velocity (\(1/\beta=c\tau/L\)). The PID cuts for inclusive electron selection are

