#### Systematic uncertainty of photonic electron \(\upsilon_{2}\)

To estimate the performance of photonic electron \(\upsilon_{2}\), we compare reconstructed electron \(\upsilon_{2}\) from both data and simulation as the reference. The reconstructed electron \(\upsilon_{2}\) from real data is calculated by the following equation with event plane \(\eta\)-sub method:

\[\begin{split}& N^{reco}\cdot\upsilon_{2}^{reco}=N^{UL}\cdot \upsilon_{2}^{UL}-N^{LS}\cdot\upsilon_{2}^{LS}\\ & N^{reco}=N^{UL}-N^{LS}\\ &\mathrm{UL}\ :\ \mathrm{unlike\ sign},\ \mathrm{LS}\ :\ \mathrm{like\ sign}\end{split} \tag{3.34}\]

As the input parent particle \(\upsilon_{2}\) are from Au+Au 62.4 GeV measurements, so first we fit the reconstructed electron \(\upsilon_{2}\) from embedding and get fitting function \(F^{reco}\). Then multiply this function with a free parameter, \(c\cdot F^{reco}\), to fit reconstructed electron \(\upsilon_{2}\) from data and determine the scale factor \(c\). Both reconstructed electron and photonic electron \(\upsilon_{2}\) would be scaled with this factor in later on analysis. We use the simulated \(\upsilon_{2}\) data

