#### 4.2.2 \(D^{0}\) decay topology

Thanks to the excellent DCA resolution provided by HFT detector, \(D^{0}\) are able to be identified by its decay topology although its life time is short (\(c\tau\simeq 123\mu m\)). Fig. 4.1 shows the \(D^{0}\) decay diagram via \(D^{0}\to K^{-}\pi^{+}\). Several topological variables are used to characterize the candidate \(K\pi\) pair topology:

* DCA\({}_{K}\) and DCA\({}_{\pi}\): DCA of \(K\)/\(\pi\) to the primary vertex.
* DCA\({}_{12}\): The closet distance between two daughter tracks.
* DecayLength: The distance between reconstructed \(D^{0}\) decay vertex and primaryvertex. \(D^{0}\) decay vertex position is defined as the middle point of the DCA position of two daugher tracks where the two tracks are closet to each other.
* DCA\({}_{D^{0}}\): DCA of reconstructed \(D^{0}\) track to the primary vertex.
* \(\cos\theta\): \(\theta\) is the angle between the direction of primary vertex pointing to the decay vertex and the direction of reconstructed \(D^{0}\) momentum. \(\cos\theta\) can be calculated by \(\sin\theta=\text{DCA}_{D^{0}}\)/DecayLength and \(\cos\theta=\sqrt{1-\sin\theta^{2}}\).

